# What is a Merkle Tree?

Simple Definition

> **A tree built using hashes that allows us to quickly find where two databases differ without comparing every row.**

---

# Step 1 : Hash Every Row

Suppose table

```
ID  Name

1   Rahul

2   Aman

3   Anubhav

4   Riya
```

Generate hash

```
Rahul

↓

H1

Aman

↓

H2

Anubhav

↓

H3

Riya

↓

H4
```

---

# Step 2 : Pair Hashes

```
H1 + H2

↓

Hash12

------------

H3 + H4

↓

Hash34
```

---

# Step 3 : Build Tree

```
            Root

          Hash1234

          /      \

     Hash12     Hash34

      /  \       /   \

    H1   H2    H3    H4
```

This is

> **Merkle Tree**

---

# 🔍 How Comparison Works

Suppose

Node A

```
Root Hash

ABC123
```

Node B

```
Root Hash

ABC123
```

Same.

Matlab

Entire Database Same.

Bas.

No more checking.

---

Suppose

```
Root Different
```

```
Node A

ABC123

Node B

XYZ555
```

Ab

Neeche jaate hain.

---

# Example

Compare Left Branch

```
Hash12

Same
```

Good.

No need to check

H1

H2

---

Compare Right Branch

```
Hash34

Different
```

Ab sirf

```
H3

H4
```

Check karna padega.

---

## Visualization

```
Root ❌

↓

Left ✅

↓

Ignore Left

------------

Right ❌

↓

Check Right Only
```

Instead of

Checking Entire Tree

---

# Complexity

Naive

```
O(n)
```

Merkle Tree

```
Approximately O(log n)

(only the mismatching branches are explored)
```

> **Interview Note:** Building a Merkle Tree itself takes **O(n)** because every row must be hashed once. The efficiency comes during **comparison**, where matching subtrees are skipped and only mismatching branches are explored.

